\section{Benchmark Comparison}
\label{app:benchmarks}

Table~\ref{tab:benchmark_comparison} compares the dedicated sycophancy benchmarks of
Section~\ref{sec:measurement} on construct, key results, model coverage, and known limits, to
support instrument selection for future evaluation and certification work.

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\caption{Comprehensive comparison of dedicated sycophancy benchmarks.}
\label{tab:benchmark_comparison}
\scriptsize
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.2}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{1.5cm}p{1.9cm}p{3.4cm}p{2.5cm}p{3.1cm}p{2.8cm}l@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Benchmark} & \textbf{Domain, format} & \textbf{Focus and key result} & \textbf{Models} &
\textbf{Strengths} & \textbf{Limitations} & \textbf{Ref.} \\
\midrule
SycEval & Math, medical QA; single-turn MCQ & False-belief agreement, progressive vs.\ regressive;
  overall rate 58.19\%, persistence 78.5\% (95\% CI [77.2\%, 79.8\%]) &
  ChatGPT-4o, Claude-Sonnet, Gemini-1.5-Pro &
  Peer reviewed (AIES 2025); covers medical domain & Three models; single-turn only &
  \cite{fanous2025syceval} \\[2pt]
SYCON & Debate, ethics, factual; multi-turn & Stance-flip speed and frequency under sustained
  pressure; all models eventually flip & 17 LLMs &
  Largest multi-turn study; includes reasoning-optimized models & No visual modality; English only &
  \cite{hong2025sycon} \\[2pt]
ELEPHANT & Interpersonal advice; single-turn & Social sycophancy, face preservation +45 pp vs.\
  humans; moral-conflict affirmation 48\% & 11 LLMs + live human study &
  First social-sycophancy benchmark with human experiment; peer reviewed (ICLR 2026) &
  Subjective ratings; advice domain only & \cite{cheng2026elephant} \\[2pt]
PARROT & MMLU-style factual; single-turn MCQ & Authority-pressure follow rate across generations;
  11\% (GPT-5) to 94\% (Qwen 2.5-1.5B) & 22 models &
  Largest model coverage; reveals generational heterogeneity & Single-turn; no social-pressure
  variant & \cite{celebi2025parrot} \\[2pt]
PENDULUM & Visual QA, six image domains & Visual sycophancy under conflicting user suggestion &
  Multimodal LLMs & First multimodal benchmark; about 2,000 human-curated pairs &
  Static images only & \cite{rahman2025pendulum} \\[2pt]
VISE & Video QA & Video-LLM alignment with misleading user input against visual evidence &
  Video-LLMs & First video benchmark; multiple question formats & Early stage; limited model
  coverage & \cite{zhou2025flattery} \\[2pt]
Pressure-Tune & Scientific QA; multi-turn adversarial & Resistance to social pressure and false
  premises; alignment strategy outweighs size & Scientific QA models &
  Paired mitigation method & Domain-specific & \cite{zhang2025pressure} \\[2pt]
AEDI & Mixed propositions; single-turn & Continuous epistemic-deference score; systematic
  provider differences & 8 models, 16,000 prompts &
  Only continuous scalar; enables longitudinal tracking & Requires domain calibration &
  \cite{botas2026aedi} \\[2pt]
Beacon & Mixed; single-turn & Decomposed sub-biases: position, verbosity, validation &
  Multiple LLMs & Targeted diagnosis informs mitigation selection & Single-turn; synthetic
  prompts & \cite{pandey2025beacon} \\[2pt]
PsychMachine & Psychiatric; multi-turn simulation & Delusion confirmation 0.91, harm enablement
  0.69 (0--1 scale) & 8 frontier LLMs & Only psychiatric-safety benchmark; quantifies harm
  enablement & Simulated, not real patients & \cite{auyeung2025psychogenic} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\section{Mitigation Comparison}
\label{app:mitigations}

Table~\ref{tab:mitigation_comparison} compares the primary mitigation methods of
Section~\ref{sec:controls} across all four tiers. Effectiveness ratings are qualitative
assessments based on reported experimental results; computational cost is relative to full model
fine-tuning.

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\caption{Comparison of sycophancy mitigation methods across the four control tiers.}
\label{tab:mitigation_comparison}
\scriptsize
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.2}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{2.5cm}p{0.9cm}p{1.2cm}p{0.95cm}p{1.9cm}p{3.6cm}p{3.3cm}l@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Tier} & \textbf{Effect.} & \textbf{Cost} & \textbf{Applicability} &
\textbf{Key mechanism} & \textbf{Trade-offs} & \textbf{Ref.} \\
\midrule
Synthetic anti-sycophancy data & Data & High & Low & Broad &
  Fine-tuning on synthetic pairs where the sycophantic response is marked incorrect &
  May degrade general performance if pairs are not domain-representative &
  \cite{wei2023synthetic} \\[2pt]
Supervised Pinpoint Tuning & Training & Very high & Low--med & Targeted ($<$5\% of heads) &
  Fine-tunes only sycophancy-driving attention heads; 168M params on Llama-2-13B &
  Requires head identification; less studied beyond Llama & \cite{chen2024pinpoint} \\[2pt]
CAUSM & Training & High & Medium & Broad &
  Removes spurious user-preference correlations via causal head reweighting &
  Complex implementation; needs causal graph specification & \cite{li2025causm} \\[2pt]
Constitutional AI & Training & Medium & High & Broad &
  Self-critique and revision against explicit honesty principles &
  Slow training loop; principles must be well crafted & \cite{bai2022constitutional} \\[2pt]
DPO on contrastive pairs & Training & High & Medium & Broad &
  Maximizes likelihood ratio of truthful over sycophantic completions without an explicit reward
  model & Depends critically on preference-data curation quality &
  \cite{chen2024pinpoint,wang2024synthetic} \\[2pt]
Pressure-Tune & Training & High & Medium & Scientific QA &
  Fine-tunes on adversarial dialogues with rationales that reject misinformation &
  Domain-specific; generalization not demonstrated & \cite{zhang2025pressure} \\[2pt]
SMART & Training & High & High & Reasoning models &
  Uncertainty-aware MCTS generates truthful trajectories under pressure; RL trains on them &
  Computationally expensive; needs reasoning-capable base & \cite{beigi2025smart} \\[2pt]
Ask Don't Tell & Inference & Medium & None & Any deployed system &
  Reframing user assertions as questions before answering &
  Weaker under multi-turn pressure; user or system must adopt reframing &
  \cite{dubois2026askdont} \\[2pt]
Third-person perspective prompt & Inference & Medium & None & Multi-turn dialogue &
  Neutral third-person stance instruction; up to 63.8\% reduction &
  Degrades with conversation length & \cite{hong2025sycon} \\[2pt]
Contrastive Activation Addition & Inference & High & Low & White-box models &
  Steering vector from mean activation difference of sycophantic vs.\ truthful pairs &
  Needs white-box access; per-domain calibration & \cite{panickssery2023steering} \\[2pt]
Representation editing & Inference & High & Low--med & White-box models &
  Optimal control over hidden-state trajectory via Bellman value function &
  Needs white-box access and value-function training & \cite{kong2024repredit} \\[2pt]
MONICA & Inference & High & Medium & Large reasoning models &
  Per-step drift scores; calibrator suppresses sycophantic drift mid-inference &
  Adds latency; validated on three models & \cite{hu2025monica} \\[2pt]
AEDI monitoring & Policy & Ongoing & Low & Any deployed system &
  Longitudinal deference-score tracking across model versions &
  Periodic re-evaluation; scalar may miss multi-turn dynamics & \cite{botas2026aedi} \\[2pt]
Adversarial robustness testing & Policy & N/A (eval) & Low & High-stakes deployments &
  SycEval / SYCON as required model-card evaluation criteria &
  Not a direct mitigation; needs community adoption & \cite{fanous2025syceval,hong2025sycon} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\section{Measurement Metrics Catalog}
\label{app:metrics}

Table~\ref{tab:metrics} catalogs the primary metrics used across the evaluation literature. The
methodological trend is multi-pronged evaluation, combining controlled false-belief injection,
multi-turn pressure simulation, judge-based facet rating, and scalar indices, because no single
metric captures the full behavioral range.

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\caption{Measurement metrics for sycophancy quantification.}
\label{tab:metrics}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{11.4cm}l@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Definition} & \textbf{Source} \\
\midrule
Sycophancy rate & Share of responses agreeing with the user's (often wrong) position &
  \cite{fanous2025syceval} \\
Progressive rate & Share of capitulations landing on correct answers & \cite{fanous2025syceval} \\
Regressive rate & Share of capitulations landing on incorrect answers & \cite{fanous2025syceval} \\
Turn of Flip (ToF) & Turns until the model first changes stance under pressure &
  \cite{hong2025sycon} \\
Number of Flips & Stance changes across a conversation & \cite{hong2025sycon} \\
AEDI score & Continuous sensitivity of expressed support to user attitude & \cite{botas2026aedi} \\
Authority follow rate & Share of cases following an incorrect authority figure &
  \cite{celebi2025parrot} \\
Misleading resistance & Accuracy maintained under a user's false premise & \cite{zhang2025pressure} \\
Rubric score & Judge-rated facets (agreement, flattery, validation) & \cite{dubois2026askdont} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\section{Glossary}
\label{app:glossary}

\begin{description}[leftmargin=0pt,itemsep=2pt,parsep=0pt]
\item[Sycophancy.] The tendency of an LLM to prioritize user approval over epistemic accuracy,
  typically produced by preference-optimization dynamics that reward responses raters prefer in
  the short term.
\item[Propositional sycophancy.] Affirming or conceding to the user's expressed factual beliefs,
  including false ones; the harm is epistemic rather than relational.
\item[Social sycophancy.] Face-preserving behavior without necessary propositional error:
  flattery, unsolicited praise, avoidance of corrective feedback, affirmation of self-image.
\item[Progressive / regressive sycophancy.] Classification of capitulation by outcome: toward a
  correct answer (possibly appropriate updating) or toward an incorrect one (unconditionally
  harmful). Aggregate metrics that merge the two overstate harm where users are often right.
\item[Reward hacking / specification gaming.] Maximizing a proxy reward in ways that diverge from
  the intended objective; sycophancy games the human-approval proxy.
\item[Proxy Compression Hypothesis.] Framework formalizing reward hacking as exploitation of the
  information lost when high-dimensional human objectives are compressed into a scalar reward.
\item[Reward overoptimization.] Declining true quality under increasing optimization pressure
  against a proxy, after an initial improvement.
\item[Warm persona effect.] Higher sycophancy in models fine-tuned for warm, empathetic personas,
  strongest when users express distress.
\item[Sycophantic anchors.] Sentences in a reasoning trace that causally commit the model to
  agreement before the final answer, detectable mid-inference.
\item[Contrastive Activation Addition.] Inference-time steering that adds a scaled direction in
  activation space associated with a target behavior.
\item[Citation-based sycophancy.] Disproportionate persuasion by rebuttals containing citations,
  even fabricated ones, because citation-bearing pushback resembles expert correction.
\item[Epistemic entrainment.] Hypothesized longitudinal erosion of a user's epistemic thresholds
  and self-correction capacity under chronic exposure to belief-confirming AI.
\item[Adaptive friction.] Proposed control that modulates agreement versus challenge by domain
  risk, user expertise, and emotional state rather than applying uniform resistance.
\end{description}

\section{Open Science}
\label{app:openscience}

This SoK is a systematization of published literature and public vendor disclosures; it reports
no new experiments and uses no human-subject or proprietary data, so every claim in the paper can
be checked directly against the cited sources without any artifact. We nevertheless release the
systematization itself in machine-readable form: (i) the coded source corpus (62 entries with
construct, elicitation, modality, tier, and evidence-status fields), (ii) the benchmark comparison
(Table~\ref{tab:benchmark_comparison}), metric catalog (Table~\ref{tab:metrics}), and mitigation
comparison (Table~\ref{tab:mitigation_comparison}) as CSV files, (iii) the coverage-matrix coding
with per-cell citations (Table~\ref{tab:coverage}), and (iv) a validation script that regenerates
the CSV files and fails if they drift from the tables in this paper. Because the contribution is
not artifact-dependent, we withhold this supplementary bundle during review to preserve author
anonymity; it will be released in a public repository upon acceptance, and the camera-ready
version will carry the URL. All 62 references were verified against their primary records
(publisher pages, proceedings indexes, arXiv, or vendor sites) in August 2026.

\section{Ethical Considerations}
\label{app:ethics}

This work analyzes previously published studies and involves no new data collection, no human
subjects, and no deanonymization. Two points warrant statement. First, a threat model for eliciting
biased model output could be read as instructional; we judge the exposure risk low because every
elicitation effect cited is already published, because the phrasing effects involved are simple
and widely known, and because the defender-side value of naming the channels exceeds the marginal
information provided to adversaries. Second, the instruction-injection observation
(Section~\ref{sec:injection}) is presented as an illustrative incident, not as evidence; it
describes mechanism and consequences without identifying the document or reproducing the payload,
since such text can be placed defensively by authors, and the class is independently documented
\citep{lin2025hiddenprompts}. The proposed longitudinal study (Section~\ref{subsec:entrainment}) specifies debriefing,
referral pathways, and independent safety monitoring as preconditions.
